---
import Prose from "../layouts/Prose.astro";
import { publisher } from "../lib/site";
---

<Prose title="Privacy" description="What data charityevals collects, why, and how to say no." updated="28 October 2026">
  <p>
    Short version: no accounts, no cookies, no third-party scripts. We count which pages and charities people look at
    without identifying anyone; if you accept, we also connect the pages of one visit using a random id kept in your
    browser. You choose when you first visit, and can <a href="#changing">change your choice</a> at any time. The assumptions you set stay in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <address>
    {publisher.name}<br />
    {publisher.street}, {publisher.postcode} {publisher.city}, {publisher.country}<br />
    <a href={`mailto:${publisher.email}`}>{publisher.email}</a>
  </address>

  <h2>Your assumptions</h2>
  <p>
    When you change an assumption or switch views, the values are saved in your browser's local storage and in the page address, so they survive reloads and you can share them as a link. They are never sent to us. Clearing your browser's site data removes them; anyone you send a link to sees the values in it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="analytics">Visitor statistics</h2>
  <p>
    We want to know which charities people look at, which assumptions they change and which donate links they follow,
    so we can improve the site. We collect this ourselves: the page sends small events to our own server, which stores
    them in Cloudflare Workers Analytics Engine. There are two levels, and you choose when you first visit.
  </p>
  <h3>Before you choose: anonymous counts</h3>
  <p>
    Each event records only what happened, on which page, and when: for example "page view, /givedirectly" or
    "assumption changed, discount rate, higher, 0.05". The events we record are page views (with the site you came
    from, by its name only, such as news.ycombinator.com), clicks on donate links, changes to assumptions (the value
    you settle on, rounded), resetting or copying your assumptions, switching between our model and the evaluator's,
    switching evaluators, opening a step of a calculation (how deep, not which), opening the full model table,
    sending the suggestion form (that it was sent, never what it said), and following a link to another site (the
    site's name and the kind of link).
  </p>
  <p>
    Nothing is stored on your device, and no identifier, IP address, browser details or location is recorded, so no
    two events can be connected to each other or to you. Because this has no effect on your privacy and serves only to
    learn how well the site works, Dutch law allows it without consent (Article 11.7a(3)(b) of the
    Telecommunicatiewet). The stored events are not personal data. If you decline, or your browser sends a Global
    Privacy Control or Do Not Track signal, we don't send even these and don't ask; we take the signal as declining,
    unless you accept here.
  </p>
  <h3>If you accept: visits connected</h3>
  <p>
    The same events, plus two random ids that mean nothing outside this site: one kept in your browser's local storage
    (so we can tell a returning browser), one for the current tab (so we can follow one visit, for example "looked at
    GiveDirectly and Against Malaria, then donated to Against Malaria"), and your country as Cloudflare sees it. We
    never learn who you are: no names, emails, IP addresses or free text are recorded, and the suggestion form's
    contents never reach these statistics. The ids are personal data, so we only use them with your consent (Article
    6(1)(a) GDPR, and Article 11.7a(1) Telecommunicatiewet). We ask again after a year.
  </p>
  <h3 id="changing">Changing your mind</h3>
  <p>
    <button type="button" class="inline-settings text-link" data-consent-open>Change your choice</button> here, at any time;
    the Privacy link at the bottom of every page leads here. Declining stops all statistics from your browser at once and deletes both ids from it, so
    nothing sent later can be linked to what was sent before. Your choice itself is kept in your browser's local
    storage so we don't ask on every page; clearing the site's data removes it and we ask again.
  </p>
  <h3>Where it's kept, and for how long</h3>
  <p>
    The events are stored by Cloudflare, as our processor, and deleted automatically after three months. Analytics
    Engine can't delete single events earlier, and we have no way to find yours without your id; withdrawing consent
    deletes the id from your browser. Only we can query the data, and we use it only to see what people use. We don't
    sell it, share it, combine it with other data or use it for advertising.
  </p>

  <h2>Hosting</h2>
  <p>
    The site is served by Cloudflare. To deliver pages and protect the site from abuse, Cloudflare processes technical data about each request, such as your IP address, browser and the page requested, and may keep it briefly in logs. We don't use this data to identify or profile visitors. Cloudflare acts as our processor under its data processing terms; where data leaves the European Economic Area, those terms provide the safeguards the GDPR requires. The legal basis is our legitimate interest in running a secure website (Article 6(1)(f) GDPR).
  </p>

  <h2>Fonts and third parties</h2>
  <p>
    Fonts are served from this site, not from a font provider. Pages load no third-party scripts. Links to GitHub, evaluators' websites and spreadsheets take you to those sites, whose own privacy policies apply.
  </p>

  <h2>Email</h2>
  <p>
    If you email us, or send a suggestion through the <a href="/suggest">suggestion form</a> (which is emailed to us; the site stores nothing), we use your address and message only to reply, and delete them when they're no longer needed for that.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Under the GDPR you can ask for access to, correction or deletion of personal data we hold about you, and object to or restrict its processing. Write to <a href={`mailto:${publisher.email}`}>{publisher.email}</a>. You can also complain to the Dutch data protection authority, the <a href="https://autoriteitpersoonsgegevens.nl">Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>If what we collect changes, we'll update this page first, and ask again if it needs your consent.</p>
</Prose>

